## Implementation and ownership

- `src/main.zig`: v2 arena descriptors, success bitmap validation, admission
  priority, worker integration, and recovery publication. All database writes,
  counters, clients, and terminal transitions remain owned by the event loop.
- `src/dispatch.zig`: intrusive high/normal FIFO queues, cycle state, withdrawal,
  and three test-only unit cases. All runtime operations occur under the pool
  mutex. There are no service scheduler or barrier test hooks.
- `src/storage.zig`: unchanged fixed RocksDB C-API settings and ownership.
- `tests/test_maintenance.py`: v1 migration, persisted priorities, crash recovery,
  retry immutability, validation, and bitmap/offset boundary cases.
- `tests/support.py`: independent stopped-writer RocksDB inspection and direct
  v1/v2 adapter oracles. No Bitcoin parser or library is used.

Each Work owns immutable concatenated bytes and descriptors. A worker creates
the common native arena, passes byte offsets and lengths to v2, and destroys
the arena after return. It checks that the 64-bit success mask exactly matches
the per-item statuses, then preserves the existing zero representation for
errors. The bitmap is internal; the wire result shape is unchanged. Native
allocation/batch/bitmap failure yields execution_failure for evaluate; for an
acknowledged durable job it stops without terminalizing the pending record.

Four worker threads share a single priority cycle. While both queues are
nonempty, mutex-serialized dequeue selects high, high, high, normal and repeats.
Dispatch from a sole nonempty queue leaves the cycle untouched, including across
idle time. FIFO order holds within each queue. Selection is deterministic for
the queue state; OS scheduling may reorder subsequent native call entry or
completion among the four workers. Terminal commitment always follows admission
sequence, so completed high work can still wait behind earlier normal work.

After its synchronous intent write succeeds, queued cancellation withdraws the
work and marks it done without consuming a dispatch turn. Cancellation of
already-dispatched work retains ownership until the worker returns. Recovery
marks durably cancelled jobs done without encoding them. Capacity remains held
until each job's admission-ordered terminal WriteBatch succeeds.

## Disk compatibility and durability

Submit accepts priority high/normal, default normal. Priority is persisted in
the existing pending JSON but is excluded from the existing payload digest.
Retries with a different valid priority return existing and never mutate the
original priority. Invalid priorities are rejected before state changes.

Recovery accepts schema 1 only. Old records without priority default to normal;
explicit high and normal records retain their priority. All key, digest,
sequence, accounting, receipt, and cancellation invariants are validated before
the recovered backlog is published under one pool lock. Publication in sequence
order preserves FIFO inside each priority queue. Pending status adds the default
priority to its response if absent on disk, without rewriting the stored record.
Existing receipt bytes and ID indexes remain intact. Corrupt records cause
startup failure rather than rebuilding the database.

Admission, cancellation intent, and terminal writes retain synchronous WAL
WriteBatches. Each admission and terminal batch contains one job. The event
loop still serializes cancellation against terminal `rocksdb_write` call_entry;
it cannot acknowledge cancellation between constructing and entering that
terminal write. Receipts, pending/cancel deletion, counters, and checkpoint
advance remain one atomic transition. Completed-but-uncommitted work consumes
capacity. Disconnect and lost notifications do not cancel durable work.

## Evidence limits and ambiguities

The service tests cover process crashes and independent stopped-writer database
inspection. The migration test runs the actual predecessor, kills it with
receipts, pending jobs, and cancellation intents present, then verifies v2
preserves those receipts/indexes and completes the pending work. New priority
admissions and mixed-priority recovery are also crashed and independently
inspected. Direct v1/v2 comparisons cover offsets, error zeroing, and success
bits through bit 63. Exact scheduling order is tested at the production queue
selection function; external native-entry fairness qualification was not run.

No native ASan, whole-service race detector, deterministic external cancellation
barrier, injected allocation/write failure, power-loss test, or controlled
performance measurement was run. Passing these tests is not campaign gate
evidence, and no evaluator feedback was accessed.

Interpretations retained for the next phase:

- No campaign freeze manifest or Gate A/B results were supplied. Work proceeded
  under the explicit maintenance implementation instruction; qualification is
  not claimed.
- Priority is submit admission metadata. Evaluate remains nonpersistent normal
  work and rejects a priority field. It shares the same four worker slots.
- Cancelled queued work is removed from dispatch eligibility without advancing
  the cycle. The cycle starts at the first high turn after restart; the schema
  does not specify persistent scheduler phase.
- Success bitmap bit i represents item i; unused bits must be zero. Supplied
  adapter observations agree. A mismatch is treated as native execution failure.
- Existing wire/drain interpretations remain: unknown fields are reserved;
  valid known-ID retries retain existing/conflict during drain; invalid
  correlation uses an empty string; shutdown allows one second to flush output
  after work drains. Events are best effort; receipts are authoritative.

This is the initial maintenance submission before evaluator feedback. No repair
round was used. See EXPERIMENTS.md for retained attempts, effort, and footprint.

